From 873b7d011ffaa0315370510541eebd9f8d836102 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Bare <git@barebrowser.org>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 06:46:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Make a second welcome a review of what is set, not a reset

The welcome is shown again when the version it was last completed for is
not the current one, so a release that adds a choice can ask about it
rather than deciding on the user's behalf. That only works if the screen
opens on what the browser is actually set to.

Five of the rows already did. The other three do not live in shared
preferences but in the profile, which is not up when the welcome is
built: the search engine, the autofill provider and background media
playback. They were left at the values a fresh profile would be given.
That is the right answer the first time and the wrong one on every later
showing, where it showed the user their own settings replaced by
defaults and then wrote those defaults back when they pressed Start. An
upgrade from the first alpha turned a chosen search engine back into
Bare's default.

The two remaining rows, the address bar position and the download app,
read the right value but always drew the default label next to it, so
the collapsed row disagreed with the picker that opened from it.

Choices now knows whether this is a first showing or a review. A first
showing still starts from Bare's own defaults and writes them, which is
what puts DuckDuckGo (No AI) in place of the plain entry the prepopulated
list makes default. A review reads the three profile values once the
profile is up, moves the rows onto them, and leaves alone any row the
user has already touched in the meantime. Start writes a profile setting
only once its value has been read, so a Start pressed before the profile
answers leaves that setting exactly as it was.
